steps.body:
A union find keeps nodes 0 to n-1 in groups. Every node stores a **parent**, and a node that is its own parent is a **root**. The root names its group. At the start every node is alone, its own root:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	n := 5
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println(parent)
}
```

```go
parent := make([]int, n)
for i := range parent {
	parent[i] = i // every node starts as its own root
}
```

To find a node's group, climb parents until you reach the root:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

{{code}}

func main() {
	parent := []int{0, 0, 1, 3}
	fmt.Println(root(parent, 2), root(parent, 3))
}
```

```go
func root(parent []int, x int) int {
	for parent[x] != x {
		x = parent[x] // climb one level
	}
	return x
}
```

To join the groups of `a` and `b`, find both roots and point one at the other. Point the **root**, never `b` itself: `parent[b] = a` would cut `b` away from the rest of its group.

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func root(parent []int, x int) int {
	for parent[x] != x {
		x = parent[x] // climb one level
	}
	return x
}

func main() {
	parent := []int{0, 1, 2, 3}
	a, b := 1, 3
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println(root(parent, 1) == root(parent, 3), parent)
}
```

```go
ra, rb := root(parent, a), root(parent, b)
if ra != rb {
	parent[rb] = ra // b's whole group joins a's
}
```

Two nodes are in the same group exactly when they have the same root. The groups never split, so union find suits questions where connections only get added.

